Title:
CPL Hook Chains

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains how Crystal Palace (CPL) supports “hook chains” to layer multiple IAT hook functions on a single Windows API (e.g., `KERNEL32!Sleep`).  
- It contrasts Crystal Kit’s older “single hook per API” approach—where multiple evasion behaviors (sleep masking + call stack spoofing) are tightly coupled in one hook—with CPL’s modular chaining model.  
- The technique registers a hook chain with `addhook "MOD$Func"` and then appends multiple hook stages in order using `attach "MOD$Func" "hook"`.  
- Each tradecraft component can live in separate object files/specs (e.g., `mask.spec`, `spoof.spec`) and be composed from a top-level loader spec, enabling easy swapping/removal without code changes.  
- This is primarily useful for red teamers and tool developers building modular evasion/loader pipelines around CPL/Beacon-style payloads.  
- It’s interesting because it turns “evasion tradecraft” into spec-driven composition, reducing maintenance and enabling rapid experimentation with different hook-based behaviors.

Technical Focus:
- IAT hooking and API redirection in Windows loaders
- Crystal Palace intrinsics (`__resolve_hook`) and spec-driven build pipelines
- Hook chaining via `addhook` and `attach`
- Modular evasion tradecraft composition (sleep masking, stack/call spoofing)
- Object/COFF/PIC/PICO style modular payload building and merging

Use Cases:
- Build modular Beacon/loader tradecraft where sleep obfuscation and call spoofing are independently swappable
- Rapidly test different evasion components by changing CPL spec files instead of recompiling/refactoring code
- Layer multiple behaviors onto high-signal APIs (e.g., `Sleep`) while keeping implementations isolated
- Create reusable “tradecraft modules” (masking, spoofing, syscall variants) that can be chained per target

Keywords:
Crystal Palace, CPL, Crystal Kit, IAT hooking, hook chains, __resolve_hook, addhook, attach, KERNEL32$Sleep, Windows API hooking, sleep obfuscation, Gargoyle, memory masking, call stack spoofing, DFR, spec files, COFF, PIC, PICO, Cobalt Strike Beacon